        "orario": "16:12",
        "timestamp": 1774710728,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. France unveils aid to shield key sectors from soaring fuel prices France Issued on: 28/03/2026 - 16:12 Modified: 28/03/2026 - 16:12 Facing soaring fuel prices from the Middle East conflict, the French government has announced €70 million in targeted aid for April, helping transport, fishing, and agriculture. Measures include a 20-cent-per-litre fuel subsidy for small transport businesses and fishing boats, plus zero excise on agricultural diesel. The support is limited, sector-focused, and adaptable, while broader tax cuts are ruled out to protect public finances. Where do French parties stand a year before the presidential election? 25/03/2026 France Emblematic MeToo trial of Islamic scholar Tariq Ramadan awaits verdict 25/03/2026 France 25/03/2026 France In Paris, enigmatic posters spark rumours of Céline Dion concerts 24/03/2026 Culture Lionel Jospin (1937-2026): 'Highly respected moral and intellectual figure among left wing' 23/03/2026 France 23/03/2026 France Dr. Andrew Smith: Elections are won by 'constructing a vision that articulates political values' 23/03/2026 France After strong showing in France's major cities, will left even qualify for 2027 presidential runoff? 23/03/2026 France Former French PM Jospin, father of 35-hour work week, dies aged 88 23/03/2026 France"
